{{Short description|Indonesian soups list}}
[[File:Soto Ayam Savoy Homann Hotel.JPG|thumb|''[[Soto ayam]]'', Indonesian counterpart of chicken soup.]]

This is a '''list of Indonesian soups'''. [[Indonesian cuisine]] is diverse, in part because [[Indonesia]] is composed of approximately 6,000 populated islands of the total 18,000 in the world's largest archipelago,<ref name="epicurina">[http://www.epicurina.com "Indonesian Cuisine."] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170823205443/http://www.epicurina.com/ |date=2017-08-23 }} [http://www.epicurina.com Epicurina.com] . Accessed July 2011.</ref> with more than [[Ethnic groups in Indonesia|600 ethnic groups]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bps.go.id/news/2015/11/18/127/mengulik-data-suku-di-indonesia.html|title=Mengulik Data Suku di Indonesia|publisher=[[Statistics Indonesia|Badan Pusat Statistik]]|access-date=12 February 2020|date=18 November 2015}}</ref> Many regional cuisines exist, often based upon [[Culture of Indonesia|indigenous culture]] and foreign influences.<ref name="epicurina"/> Indonesian soups are known to be flavoursome with generous amount of ''[[Bumbu (seasoning)|bumbu]]'' spice mixture.

Indonesian cuisine has a diverse variety of [[soup]]s.<ref name="Cornell & Anwar">{{cite book | last1=Cornell | first1=K. | last2=Anwar | first2=M. | title=Cooking the Indonesian Way: Culturally Authentic Foods Including Low-fat and Vegetarian Recipes | publisher=Ebsco Publishing | series=Easy Menu Ethnic Cookbooks 2nd Edition | year=2004 | isbn=978-0-8225-2157-0 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=o12d7eVNFZ8C&pg=PA31 | access-date=January 30, 2015 | page=31}}</ref> Some Indonesian soups may be served as meals,<ref name="Cornell & Anwar"/> while others are lighter.<ref name="Yuen">{{cite book | last=Yuen | first=D. | title=Indonesian Cooking: Satays, Sambals and More | publisher=Tuttle Publishing | year=2013 | isbn=978-1-4629-0853-0 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H0DRAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT116 | access-date=January 30, 2015 | page=116}}</ref> The [[Makassar (people)|Makassarese]] of [[South Sulawesi]], Indonesia are known for preparing "hearty beef soups"<ref name="Von Holzen Hutton Arsana">{{cite book | last1=Von Holzen | first1=H. | last2=Hutton | first2=W. | last3=Arsana | first3=L. | title=The Food of Indonesia: Authentic Recipes from the Spice Islands | publisher=Periplus Editions | series=Periplus World Food Series | year=1999 | isbn=978-962-593-389-4 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AWKM6aTEGboC&pg=PA58 | access-date=January 29, 2015 | page=58}}</ref> that also use coconut and lemongrass as ingredients.<ref name="TFOI">{{cite book | last1=von Holzen | first1=H. | last2=Arsana | first2=L. | last3=Hutton | first3=W. | title=The Food of Indonesia: Delicious Recipes from Bali, Java and the Spice Islands | publisher=Tuttle Publishing | year=2015 |page=168| isbn=978-1-4629-1491-3 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_SFFBAAAQBAJ&pg=168 | access-date=January 29, 2015}}</ref>

==Variety==
[[File:Sup Buntut - Nelayan Indonesian Restaurant.jpg|thumb|''[[oxtail soup#IIndonesian oxtail soup|Sop buntut]]'', Indonesian oxtail soup.]]
Generally Indonesian soups and stews are grouped into four major groups with numbers of variants in between. 
# '''''[[Soto (food)|Soto]]''''' refer to variety of Indonesian traditionally spiced meat soups, either in clear broth or in rich [[coconut milk]]-base soup, example includes ''[[soto ayam]]''. 
# '''''Sayur''''' refer to traditional vegetables stews, such as ''[[sayur asem]]'' and ''[[sayur lodeh]]''. 
# '''''Sop''''' or '''''sup''''' usually refer to soups derived from western influences, such as ''[[Oxtail soup|sop buntut]]''.
# '''''Mi kuah'''''  refer to various [[noodle soup]]s of Indonesia, usually refer to noodle soups derived from [[Chinese Indonesian cuisine|Chinese]] and [[Peranakan cuisine|Peranakan]] influences, such as ''[[Bakso|mi bakso kuah]]'' and ''[[laksa]]''. In Indonesia, noodles are not normally classed as soup, since the dry stir fried version of noodle is also common in the country.

This list includes soups that originated in Indonesia as well as those that are common in the country.

==Indonesian soups and stews==
{{dynamic list}}

===''Soto'' (traditional soups)===
[[File:Soto Babat 1.jpg|thumb|''Soto babat'', [[tripe]] [[Soto (food)|soto]].]]
* ''[[Soto (food)|Soto]]'' – a traditional meat soup.<ref name="CNN"/> Many variations exist.
* ''[[Soto ayam]]'' – chicken soto,<ref>{{cite web | last=Moskin | first=Julia | title=Soto Ayam (Indonesian Chicken Soup With Noodles and Aromatics) Recipe | website=New York Times Cooking | date=January 7, 2009  | url=http://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1012384-soto-ayam-indonesian-chicken-soup-with-noodles-and-aromatics | access-date=January 30, 2015}}</ref>
* ''Soto babat'' – [[tripe]] [[Soto (food)|soto]].
* ''Soto babi'' – Balinese [[pork]] [[Soto (food)|soto]].
* ''Soto daging'' – beef soup, usually eat with ''jeroan'' (offal) or quail egg satay.
* ''[[Soto padang]]'' – beef rice noodle soup with potatoes and egg, specialty of Padang. 
* ''[[Sroto]]'' or ''soto sokaraja'' – soto with peanut sambal.
* ''[[Soto Betawi]]'' – beef or chicken soup originating in West Java with a coconut milk infused broth usually eaten with emping.
* ''[[Coto makassar]]'' – [[Makassar cuisine|Makassarese]] beef soup, a traditional [[beef]] and [[offal]] ''[[Soto (food)|soto]]'' variant from Makassar, South Sulawesi.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/life/2017/02/27/coto-ranggong-a-delicious-eatery-that-harks-back-to-the-past.html|title=Coto Ranggong, a delicious eatery that harks back to the past|work=The Jakarta Post|access-date=2017-12-12|language=en}}</ref>

=== ''Sayur'' (vegetable soups) ===
[[File:Binte Biluhuta.jpg|thumb|Binte biluhuta or milu siram, a corn soup with [[skipjack tuna]] and [[shrimp]].]]
[[File:Sayur Asem Ampera.jpg|thumb|''[[Sayur asem]]'', vegetable tamarind soup.]]
* ''[[Binte biluhuta]]'' or ''milu siram'' - corn soup from [[Gorontalese cuisine]] with a unique flavour due to sweetness from the [[maize]], sourness from [[Carambola|starfruit]] and [[Lime (fruit)|lime]], and spiciness from chilies. It is enriched with cakalang ([[skipjack tuna]]) and [[shrimp]].
* ''[[Brenebon]]'' or ''sayur kacang merah'' – red [[kidney bean]] soup, served in broth made from boiled [[pig's trotters]], beef or chicken.<ref name="FAO">{{cite web | title = Recipe: Soup Brenebon | work = [[FAO]] | url = http://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/c38f8b37-352b-4d80-9d33-bfc0e9b6ccdd/}}</ref>
* ''[[Sayur asem]]'' – uses [[tamarind]] as a main ingredient, along with vegetables, [[chayote]], [[bilimbi]] and [[melinjo]]<ref name="CNN"/>
* ''[[Sayur bayam]]'' or ''sayur bening'' – spinach and corn in clear soup flavoured with ''[[Fingerroot|temu kunci]]''.
* ''[[Sayur lodeh]]'' – vegetables in [[coconut milk]] soup.
* ''[[Sayur sop]]'' – vegetables soup ([[common beans]], [[carrot]], [[cabbages]], potato, [[celery]], [[cauliflower]], fried [[shallot]]s), in chicken broth soup, often includes diced chicken.
* ''[[Sayur oyong]]'' – clear vegetable soup made of  okra or ''[[Luffa acutangula]]''.
* ''[[Pea soup|Sup ercis]]'' or ''erten'' – soup made of thick stew of green split peas, celeriac or stalk celery, onions, leeks, carrots, and often potato.
* ''[[Carrot soup|Sup wortel]]'' – soup prepared with carrot as a primary ingredient. It can be prepared as a cream or broth-style soup.

===Chicken soups===
[[File:Opor Ayam Telur Pindang.JPG|thumb|right|''[[Opor ayam]]'', chicken in coconut milk soup.]]
* ''Ayam buah keluak'' – chicken rib stew cooked with the nuts from the ''[[Pangium edule]]''.
* ''[[Opor ayam]]'' – prepared with the main ingredients of [[chicken (food)|chicken]] cooked in [[coconut milk]].<ref name="LP">Witton, Patrick; Elliott, Mark (2003), [https://books.google.com/books?id=dmDYLxcPDPoC&pg=PA108 ''Lonely Planet Indonesia'']. Lonely Planet Publications, p. 108</ref> It is especially common in [[Central Java]].<ref name="LP"/> Many additional ingredients are used.
* ''[[Chicken soup|Sup ayam]]'' – Indonesian chicken soup with a variety of vegetables, such as carrots, potatoes, cabbage and mushrooms.
* ''[[Cream soup|Sup krim]]'' – a [[cream]]y soup with addition of [[milk]] is obviously derived from western influence, and today is quite common in Indonesia. Variants include ''sup krim ayam'' (with chicken), ''sup krim jamur'' (with mushroom), and ''sup krim asparagus'' (with [[asparagus]]).

===Fish or seafood soups===
[[File:Kuah Asam Goropa (Kerapu Kuah Asam).jpg|thumb|''Ikan kerapu kuah asam'', [[grouper]] in sour soup from [[Manado]].]]
* ''[[Asam pedas]]'' – sour and spicy fish stew dish.
* ''[[Fish ball#Indonesia|Bakso ikan]]'' – fish meatball soup.
* ''Ikan kuah kuning'' – fish soup in clear yellow broth. It is a side dish of [[Papeda (food)|papeda]] and be a delicacy from [[Maluku Islands|Maluku]] and [[Papua (province)|Papua]].
* ''[[Kapurung]]'' – [[sago]] and fish soup from [[South Sulawesi]].
* ''Mangut'' – Javanese coconut milk fish soup.
* ''[[Pindang]]'' – fish boiled in salt and sour-tasting spices, usually tamarind.
* ''Pindang koyong'' – fish cooked in yellow gravy-like [[Soto (food)|soto]] with various [[Bumbu (seasoning)|spices]], specialty of [[Banyuwangi Regency|Banyuwangi]], [[East Java]].
* ''Pindang serani'' – pindang made from various kind of [[seafood]] and [[milkfish]].
* ''Pindang tongkol'' – pindang variant using pindang processed [[mackerel tuna]].
* ''Sup cakalang kuah kuning'' – [[Skipjack tuna]] in yellow spicy soup speciality of [[Manado]], North Sulawesi. Spice mixture include turmeric, ginger, candlenut, garlic, shallot, chili pepper and lemongrass.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://selerasa.com/resep-masakan-ikan-cakalang-kuah-kuning-yang-gurih|title=Resep Masakan Ikan Cakalang Kuah Kuning Yang Gurih|last=Ilham|date=2015-11-10|website=Selerasa.com|language=id-ID|access-date=2020-04-28}}</ref>
* ''Sup ikan'' – fish soup, specialty of [[Batam]] island near Singapore. Usually uses [[Mangrove red snapper|red snapper]] and dried shrimp, seasoned with shallot, garlic, pepper, soy sauce, fish sauce, add with tomato, scallion and [[bawang goreng]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://id.tastemade.com/videos/sop-ikan-batam|title=Sop Ikan Batam ~ Resep|website=Tastemade|language=id|access-date=2020-04-28}}</ref>
* ''Sup ikan kuah asam'' – seafood fish in sour soup speciality of [[Gorontalese cuisine|Gorontalo cuisine]], [[Gorontalo|Gorontalo Peninsula]] and [[Minahasan cuisine]], [[North Sulawesi]]. Various fish can be made sour soup, including grouper, trevally or red snapper.
* ''Sup krim kepiting'' – a crab creamy soup.
* ''Sup udang pedas'' – hot and spicy [[shrimp]] soup, made of shrimp, dried shrimp, fishballs, mushroom, carrot, scallion, garlic and chili pepper.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://sajiansedap.grid.id/read/10728899/sup-udang-pedas|title=Sup Udang Pedah - Sajian Sedap|website=sajiansedap.grid.id|language=id|access-date=2020-04-28}}</ref>
* ''[[Tekwan]]'' – surimi fishcake akin to [[pempek]], [[bihun]] rice noodle, jicama and mushroom soup, specialty of [[Palembang]].

===Meat and offal soups===
[[File:Sop Konro.JPG|thumb|''[[Konro]]'', spicy ribs soup.]]
[[File:Tongseng Dish.jpg|thumb|''[[Tongseng]]'', sweet and spicy goat meat soup.]]
* ''[[Bak kut teh]]'' – a pork rib dish cooked in broth, herbs, and spices.
* ''[[Bakso]]'' – a meatball soup.<ref name="CNN">{{cite web | title=40 of Indonesia's best dishes | website=CNN Travel | date=August 9, 2011| url=http://travel.cnn.com/explorations/eat/40-foods-indonesians-cant-live-without-327106 | access-date=January 30, 2015}}</ref> Meats used may include beef, pork, chicken, and mixtures of these meats.<ref name="CNN"/> Additional ingredients often include [[bok choy]], tofu, hard-boiled egg, fried shallots and wontons.<ref name="AP">{{cite news | url=http://www.nydailynews.com/life-style/eats/bakso-indonesian-meatball-soup-president-obama-loved-child-article-1.454324 | title=Bakso: The Indonesian meatball soup President Obama loved as a child | newspaper=[[NY Daily News]] | date=November 18, 2010 | access-date=30 January 2015 | agency=Associated Press }}</ref> It has been described as a national [[street food]] of Indonesia.<ref name="AP"/>
* ''Balungan'' – [[bakso]] soup that adds ''balungan'' (bone) in portions.
* ''[[Brongkos]]'' – spicy meat and beans stew, specialty of [[Yogyakarta]] and [[Central Java]].
* ''[[Empal gentong]]'' – spicy beef offal soup specialty of Cirebon, West Java.
* ''[[Feijoada]]'' – a Timorese bean soup with beef and pork, influenced by [[Portuguese cuisine]].
* ''[[Gulai]]'' – stew [[curry]] dish with main ingredients might be poultry, goat meat, beef, mutton, various kinds of offal, fish and seafood, and also vegetables such as cassava leaves and unripe [[jackfruit]].
* ''[[Kaledo (soup)|Kaledo]]'' – a traditional spicy [[cow's trotters]] soup from [[Donggala]] regency, [[Central Sulawesi]].
* ''[[Katemak]]'' – beef soup served with sweet potatoes, sweet corns, and some green vegetables such as cassava leaves and papaya leaves.
* ''[[Konro]]''<ref name="Von Holzen Hutton Arsana"/><ref name="TFOI"/> – spicy ribs soup specialty of Makassar, South Sulawesi.
* ''[[Maraq (dish)|Marak]]'' – goat meat soup dish with pumpkin and chicken meat.
* ''[[Pallubasa]]'' – spicy beef or buffalo soup specialty of Makassar, South Sulawesi.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://cookpad.com/id/resep/764252-pallu-basa-khas-makassar|title=Resep Pallu basa (khas Makassar) oleh Anna Marbun|website=Cookpad|language=id|access-date=2017-12-12}}</ref>
* ''[[Rawon]]'' – a beef soup in black ''[[Pangium edule|keluak]]'' soup that originated from [[East Java]]<ref name="CNN"/>
* ''[[Saksang]]'' – pork, dog, or water buffalo meat stew cooked in its blood, mixed with coconut milk and [[Bumbu (seasoning)|spices]].
* ''[[Saltah]]'' – stew made of [[maraq (dish)|marak]], a dollop of fenugreek froth, and ''[[sahawiq]]'' based on [[Arab Indonesian cuisine]].
* ''[[Semur (Indonesian stew)|Semur]]'' – meat stew (usually beef) with [[sweet soy sauce]] and [[Bumbu (seasoning)|spices]].
* ''[[Oxtail soup|Sop buntut]]'' – oxtail soup.<ref name="CNN"/><ref>{{cite book | last1=Wongso | first1=W. | last2=Tobing | first2=H. | title=Homestyle Indonesian Cooking | publisher=Tuttle Publishing | year=2013 | isbn=978-1-4629-1106-6 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=h5HQAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT46 | language=es | access-date=January 30, 2015}}</ref> 
* ''[[Sekba]]'' – or sometimes called ''bektim'' is a [[Chinese Indonesian cuisine|Chinese Indonesian]] [[pork]] [[offal]]s stewed in mild [[soy sauce]]-based soup.<ref name="Whitmarsh Wood 2013 p. 119">{{cite book | last1=Whitmarsh | first1=A. | last2=Wood | first2=M. | title=Jakarta: 25 Excursions in and Around the Indonesian Capital | publisher=Tuttle Publishing | year=2013 | isbn=978-1-4629-0893-6 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=nhTRAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT119 | access-date=October 1, 2016 | page=119}}</ref>
* ''Sop iga'' – an indonesian cow rib soup with celery, tomato, and carrot.
* ''[[Sop saudara]]'' – a spicy [[beef]] [[soup]] contains bits of beef and [[offal]]s (usually fried cow's lungs), [[rice vermicelli]], ''[[perkedel]]'' (fried potato patty) and hard [[boiled egg]]. The spices includes [[garlic]], [[shallot]], [[candlenut]], [[coriander]], [[caraway]], [[ginger]], [[galangal]], lime leaf, [[lemongrass]], [[nutmeg]] and [[cinnamon]]. Garnishing include chopped [[scallion]] and ''bawang goreng'' (crispy fried [[shallot]]).<ref name=tv>{{cite news|author=Usman|title=Wisata Kuliner Makassar: Menikmati Sop Saudara Dan Ikan Bandeng Bakar Khas Pangkep|url=http://www.makassartv.co.id/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=7389&catid=3&Itemid=56|date=23 March 2012|work=MakassaRTV|access-date=25 July 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305045032/http://makassartv.co.id/index.php?catid=3&id=7389&itemid=56&option=com_content&view=article|archive-date=5 March 2016|url-status=usurped}}</ref>
* ''Sop senerek'' – a traditional soup from [[Magelang]] with [[beef]], [[red bean]], [[carrot]], [[tomato]], [[celery]], and some [[fried onion|bawang goreng]].
* ''[[Sup kambing|Sop kambing]]'' – prepared with [[goat meat]], tomato, celery, [[spring onion]], ginger, [[candlenut]] and lime leaf, its broth is yellow in color.<ref name="CNN"/>
* ''Tengkleng'' – goat ''jeroan'' (offals) and ''tetelan'' (bony meat, usually from rib cage) with spicy coconut milk, specialty of Solo.
* ''Timlo solo'' – a beef and vegetable soup. Some versions also have noodles, as a [[beef noodle soup]].<ref>{{cite book | last1=Planet | first1=L. | last2=Berkmoes | first2=R.V. | last3=Brash | first3=C. | last4=Cohen | first4=M. | last5=Elliott | first5=M. | last6=Mitra | first6=G. | last7=Noble | first7=J. | last8=Skolnick | first8=A. | last9=Stewart | first9=I. | last10=Waters | first10=S. | title=Lonely Planet Indonesia | publisher=Lonely Planet Publications | series=Travel Guide | year=2010 | isbn=978-1-74220-348-5 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4GMBFsaFNN4C&pg=PA202 | access-date=January 30, 2015}}</ref>
* ''Tabu Moitomo'' or "black beef soup" - a rich black soup using beef or poultry with all 30 kinds of herbs and spices imaginable.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Recipe: Tabu Moitomo Gorontalo |url=http://omarniode.org/blogreads-362.php |access-date=2023-07-07 |website=Omar Niode Foundation}}</ref> 
* ''[[Tongseng]]'' – a sweet and spicy goat meat soup, specialty of [[Surakarta|Solo]], Central Java.

===Noodle soups===
[[File:Laksa Betawi 3.jpg|thumb|''[[Laksa#Curry laksa|Laksa betawi]]'', served with ''[[emping]]''.]]
[[File:Mie koclok.JPG|thumb|''[[Mie koclok]]'', chicken noodle soup.]]
[[File:Mie Rebus Jawa.jpg|thumb|''[[Mie kuah]]'', boiled noodles with Javanese-style.]]
* ''Bihun kuah'' – rice vermicelli soup.
* ''[[Kwetiau ayam]]'' – flat noodle soup with chicken, sometimes served with pangsit (wonton) and bakso (meatball) soup.
* ''[[Beef kway teow]]'' – flat noodle soup with slices of beef or sometimes beef offal.
* ''[[Laksa]]'' – spicy noodle soup dish which has various types based on [[Peranakan cuisine|Peranakan]]. It consists of thick wheat noodles or rice vermicelli with chicken, prawn or fish, served in spicy soup based on either rich and spicy curry coconut milk or on sour asam.
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa banjar]]'' – steamed noodle-like balls, made from rice flour paste, served in thick yellowish soup made from coconut milk, ground spices and snakehead fish broth.
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa betawi]]'' – laksa contains rice vermicelli, beansprouts, and dried shrimp [[broth]] soup.
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa bogor]]'' – laksa contains rice vermicelli, beansprouts, and [[oncom]].
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa cibinong]]'' – yellowish laksa made of coconut milk, spices, [[bean sprout]], rice vermicelli, hard-boiled eggs, cooked shredded chicken, fried shallots, and many lemon-basil leaves.
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa medan]]'' – asam laksa contains flaked [[mackerel]], wild ginger flower, lemongrass, and chili pepper.
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa tambelan]]'' – laksa made of flaked [[sauteed]] smoked [[mackerel tuna]] or [[skipjack tuna]], [[Bumbu (seasoning)|spices]] and lump of sagoo noodles, served in spicy [[kerisik]].
* ''[[Laksa|Laksa tangerang]]'' – laksa made of rice noodles shaped like spaghetti, chicken stock, [[mung beans]], potatoes and chives.
* ''[[Laksa|Lakse kuah]]'' – fish curry laksa, made of sagoo noodles with mashed tongkol or mackerel tuna flesh, served in spicy coconut milk curry made of spice mixture.
* ''[[Lakso]]'' – spicy noodle soup served in savoury yellowish coconut milk-based soup, flavoured with fish, and sprinkled with fried shallots.
* ''[[Mie ayam|Mi ayam]]'' – chicken noodle soup<ref>{{cite book | last1=Von Holzen | first1=H. | last2=Ltd | first2=M.C.I.P. | title=A New Approach to Indonesian Cooking | publisher=Marshall Cavendish International (Asia) Private Limited | year=2014 | isbn=978-981-4634-95-3 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pRuiBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA15 | access-date=September 8, 2018 | page=15}}</ref> comprising a bowl of chicken stock, boiled choy sim, celery leaves, diced chicken cooked with sweet soy sauce, and fried shallots. Some variants add mushrooms and fried/boiled pangsit (wonton). Normally it is eaten with chili sauce and pickles.<!--which vegetable is pickled?-->
* ''[[Mie bakso|Mi bakso]]'' – bakso meatballs served with yellow noodles and rice vermicelli in beef broth.
* ''[[Mie cakalang|Mi cakalang]]'' – skipjack tuna noodle soup.
* ''[[Mie celor|Mi celor]]'' – a noodle dish served in coconut milk soup and shrimp-based broth, specialty of Palembang city, South Sumatra.<ref>{{cite book | last1=Kraig | first1=B. | last2=D | first2=C.T.S.P. | title=Street Food around the World: An Encyclopedia of Food and Culture: An Encyclopedia of Food and Culture | publisher=ABC-CLIO | year=2013 | isbn=978-1-59884-955-4 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9XCjAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA184 | access-date=September 8, 2018 | page=184}}</ref>
* ''[[Curry Mee|Mi kari]]'' – soup curry noodle dish.
* ''[[Mie koclok|Mi koclok]]'' – chicken noodle soup from Cirebon. It is served with cabbage, bean sprout, boiled egg, fried onion and spring onion.
* ''[[Mie kocok|Mi kocok]]'' – (lit: "shaken noodle"), is an Indonesian beef noodle soup from Bandung, consists of noodles served in rich beef consommé soup, ''kikil'' (beef tendon), bean sprouts and bakso (beef meatball), kaffir lime juice, and sprinkled with sliced fresh celery, scallion and fried shallot. Some recipes might add beef tripe. 
* ''[[Mie kopyok|Mi kopyok]]'' – noodle soup with garlic broth, fried tofu, and rice cracker.
* ''[[Mie rebus|Mi kuah]]'' – literally "boiled noodles" in English, made of yellow egg noodles with a spicy soup gravy.
* ''Mi ongklok'' – boiled noodles were made using cabbage, chunks of chopped leaves, and starchy thick soup called ‘’loh’’. Usually served with satay and tempeh.
* ''Mi pangsit'' – noodle soup served with pangsit or soft-boiled wonton.
* ''[[Soto mie|Soto mi]]'' – noodle soup in spicy soto broth.
* ''[[Macaroni soup|Sup makaroni]]'' – Indonesian macaroni soup.

